this is the second vision. the first one is in notes/vision and it is about what
the game is. this one is about what it looks like and what your hands do, and it
arrived whole, in one piece, on 2026-08-25.

it is written down here rather than turned into documents, because a vision is
allowed to contradict the documents and the documents are not allowed to quietly
sand it down. what it collides with is listed at the bottom, as questions, not as
corrections.

--

# the vision, as it came

a tower throws spikes of bone because it's an undead commander - then the player
clicks or taps one of the stones listed underneath the tower's 3d model,
projected onto the world...

the stone is a rune, with a color. The runes are like katakana.

then, the viewpoint, which was a 3d RTS style like Warcraft or Starcraft, some of
the greats of the genere, zooms out to a grander scale, which is fully zoomed out
supreme commander scale. The places where they could place the upgrade are
highlighted, and they can also tap the other options on the tower they selected,
if they pressed the wrong button or something. These are located on the map, in
it's zoomed out state, and they can see the enemy unit armies approaching. Then,
they summon units, and they advance toward them.

--

# what is in it

Six things, and each of them is a decision even though none of them was written
as one.

**The world is three-dimensional and the camera is an RTS camera.** Warcraft,
Starcraft. Not a board seen from above.

**Upgrades live on the objects they affect, not in a panel.** They are stones
listed underneath a tower's model, projected onto the world. To touch the chest
you touch the thing the chest is doing something to.

**An upgrade is a rune, and it has a colour.** Not an icon, not a name in a list.
The runes are like katakana — a small closed alphabet of shapes that mean things,
learnable, and readable at a size where a word would not be.

**The zoom is the interface, not a convenience.** Selecting a rune zooms the
camera out to the fullest extent — Supreme Commander scale, the whole war at
once — and the act of zooming out *is* the act of asking where to put it.

**Valid destinations light up at that scale**, so a placement is chosen by
looking at the whole map rather than by reading a slot name. And the other runes
on the tower you selected stay tappable, so a wrong press costs nothing.

**And you can see the enemy armies approaching while you decide.** The placement
decision and the reason for it are on screen at the same time, which is the
opposite of a panel.

Then you summon units, and they advance toward them.

--

# what it collides with

None of these are corrections to the vision. They are places where a document
currently says something else, and the vision is the older authority.

**The viewer was settled as LÖVE**, chosen for a 2D sprite batcher drawing
thousands of near-identical bodies on a fixed view. See D1 in the open questions
and [the viewing layer](../docs/017-the-viewing-layer.md). A 3D RTS camera is a
different program with a different reason for existing, and D1's reasoning does
not survive it — the rejected alternative there was "an FFI binding to something
lower-level," rejected because "the drawing is not unusual." It is now unusual.

**The chest was a panel.** [The viewing layer](../docs/017-the-viewing-layer.md)
puts unplaced upgrades "large and impossible to ignore" in a panel, and says the
chest and the lanes should be arranged so a placement is "a short drag and not a
trip across the screen." This vision says the opposite and says it better: the
placement is not a drag at all, it is a selection and then a zoom.

**The default camera was the whole map, always**, with zoom to inspect (D7). This
vision has the default *closer* than that and the zoomed-out view arriving on
demand, as a consequence of picking something up. Those are not the same rule and
the difference matters — D7's argument was that a player must be able to judge
three lanes without doing anything, and here they do something first.

**An upgrade had no colour and no shape of its own.** It had a name and a row in
a catalogue. Runes are a real constraint on the catalogue's size — an alphabet is
learnable and a hundred symbols is not — which touches B7.

**Nothing in the documents is undead.** The setting is
[nobody remembers why](../docs/021-nobody-remembers-why.md): an ancient automated
war, machinery that still runs, libraries nobody has read. An undead commander
throwing spikes of bone fits that better than it disturbs it — *something* is
still commanding, centuries in — but the documents have never said what is doing
the commanding, and this is the first thing that has.

--

# what to do with it

Nothing yet. Phase 7 is where the viewer gets built and phase 7 is a long way
down. What this document is for is making sure that when somebody arrives there,
they arrive holding this rather than holding
[the viewing layer](../docs/017-the-viewing-layer.md)'s assumptions — which were
written before anybody had pictured the thing.

The one decision it would be a mistake to make early is the drawing library. D1
answered LÖVE on the strength of "this is thousands of small sprites on a fixed
view." If it is a 3D RTS camera at Supreme Commander scale, that sentence is
false, and the answer that followed from it should be reopened rather than
inherited.
